  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Sunday)\nIssued at 1117 AM CDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nA low chance of additional shower and thunderstorm development\nwill continue during the day Monday, mainly across parts of\nsoutheast Oklahoma and into western Arkansas, nearest the front.\nActivity tied to a second disturbance moving through the Central\nPlains, well behind the front, is expected to remain northwest of\nthe area. Trended POPs on Monday toward the short-term blended \nguidance, which depicts a faster areal and magnitude decrease as \nthe front moves away than the NBM data. Areas along and north of\nHighway 412 should see highs on Monday and Tuesday right around \nseasonal normals, while locations to the south will see \ncomparatively nicer but still well above normal temperatures. \n\nSurface high pressure in the Great Lakes region will extend\nsouthwestward into the area for the middle of the week, with the\nmain impact being a couple of mornings with true fall-feeling\ntemperatures. On Wednesday morning in particular, lower 50s may\nbe seen for areas near the Kansas border.\n\nUpper level high pressure extends northward once again into the\narea for Thursday, which should lead to a modest uptick in\ntemperatures, but with upper level trends being more progressive\nthan observed during the recent extreme heat, that feature is not\nexpected to hang around long. A period of southwest, trending to\nzonal, flow late in the week and early in the weekend should bring\nan increasing chance for showers and thunderstorms and\ntemperatures near to just above normal. Upper level ridging may\nmake a return Sunday, with just above normal temperatures \nareawide.",
